Cloud Computing Summary

Cloud Computing allows applications to use computing resources over the internet instead of relying on a single physical computer.

Key Benefits
Scalability: Easily increase or decrease resources based on demand.
On-demand self-service: Create resources quickly when needed.
Pay-as-you-go: Pay only for the resources you use.
High availability: Applications can remain available even if part of the system fails.
Global access: Applications can be accessed from anywhere.
Security: Cloud providers offer security measures to protect infrastructure.
Cloud Deployment Types
Public Cloud: Shared cloud infrastructure available to many customers.
Private Cloud: Cloud infrastructure dedicated to one organization.
Hybrid Cloud: Combination of public and private clouds.
Cloud Service Models
IaaS: Provides virtual servers, storage, and networking. The user manages the OS and applications.
PaaS: The provider manages the infrastructure and OS; the user focuses on the application.
SaaS: A complete application is provided over the internet, such as Gmail or Zoom.
Main Cloud Providers
AWS
